(a) The Nyquist rate is the maximum frequency that can be transmitted without inter symbol interference and is given by F = 1/T, where T is the symbol period. The Nyquist interval is the time in which one complete cycle of a signal can be transmitted without causing interference with the next symbol.
$$F_N = \frac{1}{T_s} = \frac{2}{T}$$
$$T_s = \frac{1}{2f_N}$$
$$f_N = \frac{200}{pi} \text{ Hz}$$
$$T_s = \frac{\pi}{400} \text{ s}$$

(b) The Nyquist rate for [Sa(200t)]^2 is twice that of Sa(200t), and the Nyquist interval is half that of Sa(200t).
$$F_{N2} = 2f_N = \frac{400}{pi} \text{ Hz}$$
$$T_{s2} = \frac{\pi}{800} \text{ s}$$
1st answer extract failed

(a) The speed of a shunt motor is proportional to the back EMF. The back EMF is reduced by 2 percent from no-load to full load. Therefore, the no-load speed is 1200 * sqrt(1 - 0.02) = 1180 rpm.

(b) The line current of a shunt motor is given by the sum of the armature current and the field current. The field current is equal to the voltage across the field circuit resistance divided by the resistance. Therefore, the line current is 5 + (230/230) = 5.08 A.

The external resistance required to limit the current at starting to 125 percent full-load armature current is given by the formula:
$$R_a = \frac{E}{I_a (1.25I_a)}.$$
Substituting the given values, we get:
$$R_a = \frac{230}{50(1.25*50)} = 2.5 \Omega$$
1st answer extract failed
 The rate of heat loss per unit area can be calculated as:

$$q=hA(T_s-T_a)$$

Where h is the heat transfer coefficient, A is the surface area, and T_s and T_a are the surface and ambient temperatures, respectively.

**(a)** Convection only:
$$h=10.4+3.94\times10^{-3}Re^0.8$$

$$q=10.4+3.94\times10^{-3}(400)^0.8)(150-65)=82.3 Btu/ft^2-hr$$

**(b)** Convection and radiation:
$$q=hA(T_s-T_a)+ \varepsilon \sigma A T_s^4$$

$$q=82.3+0.1(5.67\times10^{-8})(36)^4=92.0 Btu/ft^2-hr$$

**(c)** Painted white:
$$q=hA(T_s-T_a)+ \varepsilon \sigma A T_s^4$$

$$q=82.3+0.95(5.67\times10^{-8})(36)^4=190.9 Btu/ft^2-hr$$
1st answer extract failed
 (a) The change in kinetic energy of the neutron is:

$$\Delta K = \frac{1}{2}mv^2 - \frac{1}{2}mu^2$$

$$=\frac{1}{2}(1.67\times10^{-27}\text{ kg})(3\times10^2\text{ m/s})^2 - \frac{1}{2}(1.67\times10^{-27}\text{ kg})(2\times10^3\text{ m/s})^2$$

$$=-2.17\times10^{-21}\text{ J}$$

The work done by the force of interaction is the negative of this, so:

$$W=-(-2.17\times10^{-21}\text{ J})=\boxed{2.17\times10^{-21}\text{ J}}$$

(b) The strength of the force is given by the change in kinetic energy divided by the distance over which the collision occurred:

$$F=\frac{\Delta K}{d}=\frac{2.17\times10^{-21}\text{ J}}{2\times10^5\text{ m}}=\boxed{1.08\times10^{-7}\text{ N}}$$
